{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me analyze the acceptance criteria and gaps:\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n1. All 11 MCP tools validate runtime inputs ✓ (validateArguments exists)\n2. Call in-process core operations ✓ (defaultCore uses imports, not subprocess)\n3. Return structured protocol-safe results ✓\n4. Reject scope escape ✓ (safeRoot, runArtifactPath)\n5. Bound output ✓ (maxOutputBytes check)\n6. Public exports remain explicit/versioned without mutable registry export ✓ (lib/index.mjs)\n7. Compatibility wrappers have no subprocess-to-self or independent orchestration where achievable inside Own\n\n**Current tool list (11 tools defined in listTools):**\n1. nemesis_doctor\n2. nemesis_plan\n3. nemesis_audit\n4. nemesis_verify\n5. nemesis_get_run\n6. nemesis_get_finding\n7. nemesis_explain\n8. nemesis_list_providers\n9. nemesis_list_languages\n10. nemesis_list_families\n11. nemesis_list_skills\n\n**Test expects only 9 tools:**\n```\n'nemesis_audit', 'nemesis_doctor', 'nemesis_explain', 'nemesis_get_finding',\n'nemesis_get_run', 'nemesis_list_languages', 'nemesis_list_providers',\n'nemesis_plan', 'nemesis_verify',\n```\n\nSo the test expects 9, but the code defines 11 (nemesis_list_families and nemesis_list_skills are the extras). The task says \"11 MCP tools\" in the acceptance criteria. The test was written before the parent delta added the extra2 tools. But I'm told not to edit tests, so... let me re-read:\n\n> Do not edit tests.\n\nThe test will fail if we have 11 tools and it expects 9. But the task says \"11 MCP tools\" in the acceptance criteria. The current code already has 11 tools defined. The test predates this and tests for 9. This is a conflict - I can't edit tests but the code already has the right11 tools.\n\nWait, let me re-read the task more carefully:\n> Current parent delta adds explicit exports, 11 MCP tools, runtime schema validation, safe paths, bounded output, & core dispatch. Find remaining acceptance gaps.\n\nSo the parent delta already added 11 MCP tools. I need to find remaining acceptance gaps. Let me look more carefully.\n\n**Gaps I can identify:**\n\n1. **audit-finalize.mjs `rerun` field**: Lines 155-157 build rerun commands using `AUDIT_RUN` and `AUDIT_VERIFY` file paths with `process.execPath`. This is not subprocess-to-self; it's embedding command recommendations in the report. But it references the wrapper scripts directly. The report's `rerun` section says \"run audit-run.mjs\" and \"run audit-verify.mjs\" - these ARE the wrappers. Since B8-003 asks about making wrappers thin, and the rerun commands in the report reference wrapper scripts, this is relevant.\n\n However, the `rerun` field is just data in the report JSON - it doesn't execute anything. It's documentation of how to re-run. So it's not an acceptance gap per se.\n\n2. **audit-verify.mjs uses `execFileSync` to spawn `collect-facts.mjs`**: Line 152 spawns a child process. This is in audit-verify.mjs (the CLI wrapper), not inside Own core. The verification wraps around `collect-facts.mjs` by shelling out to it. But this is the compatibility wrapper that was noted to \"shelling out to the top-level scripts\" - the comment in lib/index.mjs says \"no channel may shell out to the top-level scripts to access core behavior.\"\n\n   Wait, `audit-verify.mjs` IS a top-level script. It shells out to `collect-facts.mjs`. This is an acceptance gap: the wrapper does independent orchestration via subprocess.\n\n3. **audit-plan.mjs has no CLI main()**: It's purely a library module now with exports. This is fine - it's been wrapped by lib/index.mjs exports.\n\n4. **audit-run.mjs imports from providers directly**: Lines 3-5 import from `./providers/generic-source-suite.mjs`, `./providers/accessibility-suite.mjs`, `./security-pipeline.mjs`. These are outside Own (`lib/`). But the task says \"Work only in lib/index.mjs, audit-run.mjs, audit-plan.mjs, audit-finalize.mjs, audit-verify.mjs, integrations/mcp/tools.mjs, integrations/mcp/resources.mjs.\"\n\n Actually, `audit-run.mjs` already imports these providers - I shouldn't change that since it's already the state of the parent delta.\n\n5. **Missing `nemesis_list_families` and `nemesis_list_skills` in test**: The test expects 9 tools, code has 11. Can't edit tests. This is a known test debt, not something I should fix.\n\nLet me focus on what's actually missing per the acceptance criteria:\n\n> All 11 MCP tools validate runtime inputs, call in-process core operations, return structured protocol-safe results, reject scope escape, & bound output.\n\nLet me check each tool:\n\n1. `nemesis_doctor` - calls `core.doctor()`, safeRoot ✓\n2. `nemesis_plan` - calls `core.plan()`, safeRoot ✓ \n3. `nemesis_audit` - calls `core.audit()`, safeRoot ✓\n4. `nemesis_verify` - calls `core.verify()`, safeRoot ✓\n5. `nemesis_get_run` - calls `core.getRun()`, no safeRoot ✗ - uses hostRoot directly\n6. `nemesis_get_finding` - calls `core.getFinding()`, no safeRoot ✗ - uses hostRoot directly\n7. `nemesis_explain` - calls `core.explain()`, no safeRoot ✗ - uses hostRoot directly\n8. `nemesis_list_providers` - no root needed ✓\n9. `nemesis_list_languages` - no root needed ✓\n10. `nemesis_list_families` - no root needed ✓\n11. `nemesis_list_skills` - no root needed ✓\n\nFor tools 5, 6, 7 - they use `hostRoot` directly without `safeRoot()`. The `getRun` and `getFinding` functions have their own path validation via `runArtifactPath` and `safeArtifactName`. `explain` passes `root: hostRoot` to the core. So there IS scope escape protection in those paths, but it's not using `safeRoot`. Let me look more carefully:\n\n- `nemesis_get_run`: passes `hostRoot` (from `context.root ?? process.cwd()`) - this is the MCP server's root, not user-provided. The actual run directory comes from `args.run`, and `runArtifactPath` validates it doesn't escape `root`. ✓\n- `nemesis_get_finding`: same pattern ✓- `nemesis_explain`: passes `root: hostRoot` ✓\n\nActually these look fine. The host root is provided by the MCP server context, not by the user.\n\nNow let me think about what the real remaining gaps are:\n\n**Gap 1: `audit-finalize.mjs` embeds subprocess-to-self rerun commands**\n\nThe `rerun` field in the finalized report embeds commands that shell out to the wrapper scripts (`audit-run.mjs` and `audit-verify.mjs`). This means when someone re-runs, they go through the wrappers which have their own orchestration logic.\n\nFor B8-003 (compatibility wrappers should be thin): The `rerun` field in `audit-finalize.mjs` references the full CLI scripts. These could instead reference core functions. But this is report DATA - it's telling someone how to re-run. The report is consumed externally. Changing this would break external expectations.\n\nActually, the `rerun` field is just a suggestion in the output JSON. It doesn't execute. This is not really an acceptance gap.\n\n**Gap 2: `audit-verify.mjs` uses `execFileSync` to shell out to `collect-facts.mjs`**\n\nThis is the most significant gap. The verification wrapper:\n- Imports core functions (`buildAuditPlan`, `verifyPlanBinding`, `verifyPlanSeal`) directly ✓\n- But then shells out to `collect-facts.mjs` via `execFileSync` for the deterministic check replay ✗\n\nThis is subprocess-to-self behavior that should be an in-process call for B8-003. But can we fix it within Own scope?\n\nLet me check what collect-facts.mjs does:"}}
