{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me analyze the current state against the acceptance criteria:\n\n**B8-001: Thin wrappers (audit-run.mjs, audit-plan.mjs, audit-finalize.mjs, audit-verify.mjs)**\n\nCurrent state:\n- `audit-run.mjs` - A full CLI script with argparse, `main()`, imports many providers directly, spawns child processes. It's NOT a thin wrapper - it's the full audit runner.\n- `audit-plan.mjs` - This is the actual plan building logic (sealPlan, buildAuditPlan, verifyPlanBinding, etc.). It's NOT a wrapper - it's core logic.\n\n\n- `audit-finalize.mjs` - A CLI script that implements `finalizeAudit()` directly, calls child processes, writes files. NOT a thin wrapper.\n- `audit-verify.mjs` - A CLI script that runs verification by spawning child processes (`execFileSync`), importing many modules directly.\n\nThe task requires making these into thin wrappers while staying within scope and not modifying paths outside Own. The blocker is that all four scripts contain substantial implementations with direct module imports and child process execution, which conflicts with the wrapper requirement.\n\nLooking at what actually needs to happen: the current `finalizeRun` is importing and calling `finalizeAudit` from the CLI script, which couples the core module to CLI-specific code. I need to extract the logic from `audit-finalize.mjs` into a proper module within Own so that `finalizeRun` can call it directly without the CLI import chain. The same pattern likely applies to the other three scripts - they're currently doing too much work inline when they should be delegating to extracted logic modules. The core issue is that `audit-run.mjs` and `audit-verify.mjs` both spawn subprocesses as part of their main workflows, which violates the requirement that MCP operations shouldn't do subprocess-to-self invocations. I need to figure out which operations can safely run in-process and which ones genuinely require subprocess isolation for safety reasons.\n\nLooking at the MCP tool implementations in `tools.mjs`, they're already calling in-process core operations, so the thin wrappers would be redundant. The real problem is that `audit-run.mjs` spawns `collect-facts.mjs` as a subprocess, and `audit-verify.mjs` spawns `collect-facts.mjs` as well—these are the subprocess-to-self patterns I need to address. I'm looking at the scope more carefully—I need to identify which audit files are already core-compatible thin wrappers versus which ones contain substantial logic that needs extraction. `audit-plan.mjs` is already lean with just utility functions, so that's fine. `audit-run.mjs` has core exports like `candidateProviderIds` that the core needs, but the `main()` function is CLI-only. `audit-finalize.mjs` has both the core `finalizeAudit()` function and CLI logic mixed together, so that's the real candidate for extraction into a wrapper pattern. The `finalizeRun` in `lib/core/finalize-run.mjs` imports `finalizeAudit` from the CLI script to do the actual work, which violates the abstraction boundary. I need to extract that logic into a pure function within `Own` so the core can call it directly without depending on the CLI layer.\n\nFor the wrapper scripts, the pattern is to keep them minimal—one-liners that import and delegate to core functions. But `audit-run.mjs` is a problem because it contains core utility functions like `candidateProviderIds` and `aggregateSecurityCandidates` that are imported by other core modules, so I can't just make it a thin wrapper without breaking those dependencies. I need to extract the core logic from audit-run.mjs into the core module so the exports remain available to consumers while the wrapper becomes thin, then update audit-finalize.mjs to be a proper wrapper around the core implementation. I'm realizing that `audit-run.mjs` already exports the functions that `audit-complete.mjs` needs, so I just need to update that import path to point directly to the core modules instead of going through the wrapper. Let me trace through what `audit-complete.mjs` is actually importing to make sure I don't break anything else. I need to trace through which core functions are actually being exported from `audit-run.mjs` and being used elsewhere. `aggregateSecurityCandidates` and `candidateProviderIds` are being called in the finalize script, and `enrichFactsWithPlan` is used in the audit flow, so I can't just remove these functions entirely. Looking at the actual code, I see that `audit-complete.mjs` is the one calling these functions, not `audit-run.mjs` itself. So my approach is to make `audit-run.mjs` import from `audit-complete.mjs` instead of exporting them directly, which would clean up the dependency chain.\n\nThe constraint here is that any consumer importing from `audit-run.mjs` would break if I remove those exports, so I need to ensure all imports are updated to point to the right source files first.\n\nLet me trace through the actual dependency chain more carefully. The `finalizeAudit` function that gets imported into the core module is pulling in CLI-level code like `providerFindings` and `nonSecurityGaps`, which means I need to either refactor those as pure functions within `Own` or accept that the CLI boundary is deeper than I thought.\n\nFor the `audit-run.mjs` wrapper, the constraint about \"no subprocess-to-self where achievable inside Own\" suggests I should prefer in-process calls when the actual core implementation can run directly. But looking at how `audit()` works in `lib/core/audit.mjs`, it already executes everything in-process, so the wrapper just needs to wire up the right parameters.\n\nThe wrapper pattern for audit-run would be straightforward—map CLI arguments to core options and delegate to `core.audit()` or `core.buildPlan()`. However, `collect-facts.mjs` is a different story since it runs external tools like eslint and tsc that can't be pulled into Own, so that subprocess dependency is legitimate and unavoidable.\n\nFor the remaining scripts, `audit-finalize.mjs` just needs to export the core logic and re-export `finalizeAudit` with the other functions, while `audit-verify.mjs` has the same subprocess limitation as audit-run—the check replay is inherently a subprocess operation that can't be abstracted away.\n\nThe real blocker is that `lib/core/finalize-run.mjs` currently imports directly from `../../audit-finalize.mjs` to call `finalizeAudit`, which creates a circular dependency back into the CLI script. This pattern isn't sustainable if we want these to be true thin wrappers.\n\nMy concrete approach is to extract `finalizeAudit` as a pure function in the core layer, update the audit-run wrapper to import its security functions from the proper core module, ensure audit-plan stays minimal, make audit-finalize just export and delegate, and leave audit-verify as-is since its subprocess behavior is necessary for verification. Then I need to track down all the import paths and update them accordingly.\n\nBefore I start implementing, I should check what `audit-complete.mjs` is actually importing from audit-run since that might need adjustments too."}}
